.TH QSNAP 1 "pipe"
.CT 1 filmprinter graphics
.SH NAME
qsnap \(mi high resolution digital film printer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qsnap
[
.BI -ncbdfmFRrs
]
[
.I N ...
]
.I file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Qsnap
produces images on a QCR digital film printer.
Each input
.I file
should be in the form of
.IR picfile (5),
3-channel for color or 1-channel for black and white;
more than one
.I file
may be given, and they are tiled onto the same exposure in the
order given.
.LP
Option letters appear in one string.
Certain options require a numeric argument,
.I N,
which follows the string as a separate argument, in the same order
as the option letters that need one.
.TP
.B n
Read a headerless, square, black-and-white raster
(a
.IR picfile (5)
with no header); the default.
.TP
.B c
Interpret each
.I file
as a multi-channel, td-format color
.IR picfile (5)
image, rather than the default headerless black-and-white raster.
.TP
.B b N
Set the brightness of the image to
.I N,
.RI 0\(<= N \(<=9
.RI ( N
is taken mod 10).
The default brightness value is 2 in
high resolution mode (4K\(mu4K pixels), and 0 in
low resolution mode (2K\(mu2K pixels).
.RI ( N=9
leaves the brightness calibration unchanged instead of setting it.)
.TP
.B d N
Dither the image by \(+-N,
.RI 2\(<= N \(<=255;
the sign of
.I N
is ignored.
.TP
.B f N
Correct the exposure for a given type of film;
.I N
is taken mod 8, and
.I N=6
is rejected as an unknown film type.
.br
0 = (default) no correction.
.br
1 = linear correction table.
.br
2 = Polaroid Type 559 color pack film, 2K mode.
.br
3 = Ektachrome 100 color film, 2K mode.
.br
4 = Ektachrome 100 color film, 4K mode.
.br
5 = Polaroid Type 559 color pack film, 4K mode.
.br
7 = Polaroid Type 809 color film, 4K mode.
.LP
Tmax-100 black & white film is defined as film type 8, but since
.I N
is taken mod 8 before use,
.B "\-f 8"
silently behaves as
.B "\-f 0"
instead; there is no way to select it through this option as
.I Qsnap.c
is currently written.
.TP
.B m N
Set the enlargement factor.
The image is enlarged with a simple box filter.
Default values are
.IR N =3
for 2K resolution and
.IR N =6
for 4K.
.TP
.B F N
Select the black-and-white exposure filter,
.RI 0\(<= N \(<=3
.RI ( N
is taken mod 4): 0 = neutral, 1 = red, 2 = green, 3 = blue.
The default is 2 (green).
.TP
.B R
Rotate the image 90 degrees clockwise before exposing it.
Takes no argument; the resolution itself is detected automatically
from the printer hardware and is not settable by an option.
.TP
.B r
Expose the red channel of a color image only (for multiple
red overlays); has no effect unless combined with
.BR c .
.TP
.B s N
When more than one
.I file
is given, skip the first
.I N
of them.
.LP
The imaging resolution for 35mm film is 114 pixels/mm (2895 dots/inch)
in high resolution mode, and 57 pixels/mm in low resolution mode.
The maximum size image that fits a 35mm negative is 3840x3072 pixels.
It takes about 2 minutes to render such an image in black and white,
or 6 minutes in color.
Since the color film is usually less sensitive to red, it is good
practice to expose the red channel of a color image twice, using
option
.BR r .
Kodak Ektachrome color film, 100 ASA, or
Kodak Tmax-100 black and white film are recommended.
For 100 ASA film, imaging at brightness level 2 produces
the best results.
.LP
With the 4\(mu5 inch module, the imaging resolution is 34 pixels/mm
(864 dots/inch) in high resolution mode, 17 pixels/mm in low resolution.
Polaroid Type 559 color film or Polaroid Type 52
black and white film is recommended.
.LP
With the 8\(mu10 inch module, the imaging resolution is 17 pixels/mm.
This module can only be used in high resolution mode.
Polaroid Type 809 color film is recommended.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
